WEBVTT

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:02.540
Another speeding event needs a

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:03.310
different response.

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.250
Learn how extra speed changes

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.010
control, and choose safer

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.760
actions before hazards develop.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.050
Stopping starts before braking

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:12.600
begins.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.210
Your truck continues moving

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.780
during perception and reaction,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.800
then through brake response and

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:18.980
the final slowing phase.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.570
Ten percent faster adds energy.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:24.070
With the same truck and

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.250
load, kinetic energy rises by

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.840
twenty-one percent, making

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.050
control more demanding.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:31.950
Stopping distance depends on

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:32.700
more than energy:

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.850
reaction time, brake response,

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:35.670
tires,

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.800
available traction and changing

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.310
road conditions all matter.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:42.350
Curve speed affects rollover

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:42.720
risk.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.260
A high center of gravity

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.170
increases that risk; reduce

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.030
speed before entry and maintain

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:48.810
a steady path.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.840
Prepare before the downhill

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:52.410
begins.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.620
Choose safe entry speed and

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.520
gear using the manufacturer's

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.810
guidance and your fleet's

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.170
approved descent procedure.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:01.980
Heat can reduce braking

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:02.550
effectiveness.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.810
Entering too fast and relying

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.020
on prolonged service braking can

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.450
leave less reserve for the

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:09.020
next hazard.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:11.830
Rain reduces visibility;

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.500
traffic bunches ahead.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:15.330
Ease off early and rebuild

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:15.730
space,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.880
then discuss any delay with

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.380
dispatch when safely parked.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.230
Change the decision before the

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:22.580
hazard.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.500
Identify your pressure point,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.020
choose an early action,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.920
and arrange schedule changes

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.000
after parking safely.
